          %h2#definitions Definitions
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong reflexive pair
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+pair"} parallel pair
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g\colon A\rightrightarrows B
              having a common
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s\colon B\to A
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi s
                      %mo =
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi s
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ s = g \circ s = 1_B
              %strong reflexive coequalizer
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
              of a reflexive pair. A category
              %strong has reflexive coequalizers
              if it has coequalizers of all reflexive pairs.
            %p
              Dually, a reflexive coequalizer in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}
              is called a
              %strong coreflexive equalizer
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Reflexive coequalizers should not be confused with
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+coequalizers"} split coequalizers
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruence"} congruence
              is a reflexive pair, so in particular any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+object"} quotient
              of a congruence is a reflexive coequalizer.
          %h2#properties Properties
          #LintonTheorem.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^T
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Moore+category"} Eilenberg Moore algebras
              is cocomplete if and only if it has reflexive coequalizers. This is the case particularly if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              preserves reflexive coequalizers.
          %p
            This is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Linton"}> Linton
            ).
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^T
              has reflexive coequalizers. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^T
              certainly has coproducts, because if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_i
              is a collection of
              = succeed "-algebras," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^T
              of the reflexive pair
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi i
                    %mi F
                    %mi U
                    %mi F
                    %mi U
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %munderover
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mrow
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                          %mi i
                        %mi F
                        %mi U
                        %mi ε
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %munder
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                          %mi i
                        %mi ε
                        %mi F
                        %mi U
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mi i
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi i
                    %mi F
                    %mi U
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\sum_i F U F U A_i
                    \\underoverset
                    {\sum_i F U \varepsilon A_i}
                    {\sum_i \varepsilon  F U A_i}
                    {\rightrightarrows}
                    \\sum_i F U A_i
            %p using the fact that the displayed coproducts exist because, for example,
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi i
                    %mi F
                    %mi U
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi i
                    %mi U
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_i F U A_i \cong F(\sum_i U A_i)
            %p
              since the left adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              preserves coproducts, assumed to exist in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_i A_i
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^T
              is routine.
            %p Finally, a category with coproducts and reflexive coequalizers is cocomplete. It suffices that general coequalizers exist, but it is easily seen that if
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, g \colon A \stackrel{\to}{\to} B
            %p is a parallel pair, then the coequalizer of the reflexive pair
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi g
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mn 1
                            %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mn 1
                            %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A + B \stackrel{\overset{(f, 1_B)}{\to}}{\underset{(g, 1_B)}{\to}} B
            %p
              (note both maps are retracts of the inclusion
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo +
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to A + B
          #PreservingReflectiveCoequalizersInTwoVariables.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo ×
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon C\times D\to E
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              of two variables which preserves reflexive coequalizers in each variable separately (that is,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(c,-)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(-,d)
              preserve reflexive coequalizers for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
              and
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\in D
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              preserves reflexive coequalizers in both variables together.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              This is emphatically
              %em not
              the case for arbitrary coequalizers.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              %strong
                of proposition
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PreservingReflectiveCoequalizersInTwoVariables"}
              Suppose given two reflexive coequalizers
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_0 \stackrel{\to}{\to} c_1 \to c_2
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0 \stackrel{\to}{\to} d_1 \to d_2
            %p
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{i j}
              denote
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(c_i, d_j)
              so that we have a diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mrow
                                %mn 0
                                %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mrow
                                %mn 0
                                %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mrow
                                %mn 0
                                %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mrow
                                %mn 1
                                %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mrow
                                %mn 1
                                %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mrow
                                %mn 1
                                %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mrow
                                %mn 2
                                %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mrow
                                %mn 2
                                %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi c
                              %mrow
                                %mn 2
                                %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    c_{0 0} &amp; \stackrel{\to}{\to} &amp; c_{0 1} &amp; \to &amp; c_{0 2} \\
                    \\downarrow \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \downarrow \\
                    c_{1 0} &amp; \stackrel{\to}{\to} &amp; c_{1 1} &amp; \to &amp; c_{1 2} \\
                    \\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \\
                    c_{2 0} &amp; \stackrel{\to}{\to} &amp; c_{2 1} &amp; \to &amp; c_{2 2}
                    }
            %p
              in which all rows and columns are reflexive coequalizers (using preservation of reflexive coequalizers in separate variables), and all squares are
              %em serially
              commutative. According to
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#BarrWells"} Toposes, Triples, Theories
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "#Johnstone"} Topos Theory
          %p
            Proposition
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PreservingReflectiveCoequalizersInTwoVariables"}
            is particularly interesting when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of a cocomplete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+monoid+monad"} free monoid monad
            on such a category preserves reflexive coequalizers, and thus (by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#LintonTheorem"} Linton’s theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+objects"} monoid objects
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is cocomplete.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Reflexive coequalizers in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits+commuting+with+colimits"} commute
              with
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
            %p
              the
              = succeed "-fold" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^n \stackrel{\prod}{\to} Set
              preserve reflexive coequalizers.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              This follows from prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PreservingReflectiveCoequalizersInTwoVariables"}
              as well as from the fact that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
              category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %munder
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi d
                              %mn 1
                        %mover
                          %mo ←
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi s
                              %mn 0
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mn 0
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ 0 \stackrel{\overset{d_0}{\to}}{\stackrel{\overset{s_0}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{d_1}{\to}}} 1\}
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0 \circ s_0 = d_1 \circ s_0 = id
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sifted+category"} sifted category
          %p
            Of course, the diagonal functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta: Set \to Set^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            to the product functor, preserves reflexive coequalizers; therefore the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi Δ
                  %mi Set
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set \stackrel{\prod \Delta}{\to} Set: x \mapsto \hom(n, x)
          %p also preserves reflexive coequalizers.
          %p This has a further consequence which is technically very convenient:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitary+monad"} finitary monad
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              preserves reflexive coequalizers.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              We have a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
              formula for
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Fin
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(-) \cong \int^{n \in Fin} T(n) \times \hom(n, -)
            %p
              and since this is a colimit of functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(n, -)
              which preserve reflexive coequalizers,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              must also preserve reflexive coequalizers.
          %p
            Since finitary monads
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            preserve reflexive coequalizers, it follows that the monadic functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \colon Set^T \to Set
            reflects reflexive coequalizers, and so since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            has reflexive coequalizers,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^T
            must as well. Therefore, by proposition
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LintonTheorem"}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^T
            is cocomplete. This is actually true for infinitary monads
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            as well, at least if we assume the axiom of choice (see
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/colimits+in+categories+of+algebras#reflexive_coequalizers_and_cocompleteness_16"} here
            for a proof), but the argument just given is a choice-free proof for the case of finitary monads.
          %h2#applications Applications
          %ul
            %li
              Reflexive coequalizers figure in the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crude+monadicity+theorem"} crude monadicity theorem
          %h2#see_also See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+reflexive+coequalizer"} local reflexive coequalizer
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Linton
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Linton"}> Fred Linton
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Coequalizers in categories of algebras
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Seminar on Triples and Categorical Homology Theory
                %strong 80
                (1969) 75-90 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0083082"}> doi:10.1007/BFb0083082
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"}> Michael Barr
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Wells"} Charles Wells
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toposes%2C+Triples%2C+and+Theories"} Toposes, Triples, and Theories
                %strong 278
                Springer (1983), republished in: Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories
                %strong 12
                (2005) 1-287 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/12/tr12abs.html"} tac:tr12
                in
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TAC+reprints+series"} TAC reprints series
            %li#Johnstone
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topos Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 10
          %p
            A description of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+cocompletion"} free cocompletion
            of a category with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+coproducts"} finite coproducts
            under reflexive coequalisers by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Pitts"} Andrew Pitts
            is given in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marta+Bunge"}> Marta Bunge
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aurelio+Carboni"} Aurelio Carboni
              %em The Symmetric Topos
              , JPAA
              %strong 105
              (1995) pp.233-249.
          %p
            They furthermore claim that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+cocompletion"} free cocompletion
            of a category under all small colimits is given by the free cocompletion under reflexive coequalisers of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+coproduct+completion"} free coproduct completion
            of the category (see the end of §2). However, this claim is not proven, and seems dubious, considering that reflexive coequalisers do not commute with infinite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Ad%C3%A1mek"}> Jiří Adámek
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Václav Koubek
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/V%C3%A1clav+Koubek"} ?
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Jiří Velebil
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Velebil"} ?
              = succeed "," do
                %em A duality between infinitary varieties and algebraic theories
